the old override syntax.

212
00:14:47,407 --> 00:14:53,183
So in the interim, while this is
changing this INITSCRIPT_NAME:${PN},

213
00:14:53,183 --> 00:14:57,848
in previous builds of Yocto,
this was actually an underscore.

214
00:14:57,848 --> 00:15:00,020
And so, while we're in the transition,

215
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:03,879
you might find documents that reference
either a colon or an underscore.

216
00:15:03,879 --> 00:15:07,662
We'll use colon going forward for
this course and

217
00:15:07,662 --> 00:15:11,634
all the base assignment
content that we're using.

218
00:15:14,478 --> 00:15:18,237
All right, and then once you have
your package defined, building and

219
00:15:18,237 --> 00:15:22,009
running in a part of your layer,
you'll want to add that to your image.

220
00:15:22,009 --> 00:15:25,432
And what this comes down
to is just simply adding

221
00:15:25,432 --> 00:15:31,009
a CORE_IMAGE_EXTRA_INSTALL+= on
your core-image-aesd.bb file.

222
00:15:31,009 --> 00:15:35,483
So our core-image-aesd is where we're
going to define the content of the image.

223
00:15:35,483 --> 00:15:39,337
And removing the comment here
on CORE_IMAGE_EXTRA_INSTALL,

224
00:15:39,337 --> 00:15:43,601
we'll just mean that we'll bring in
that recipe that we just defined core

225
00:15:43,601 --> 00:15:45,610
aesd-assignments in our layer.

226
00:15:45,610 --> 00:15:50,240
And you can space separate on this line or
add multiple lines to add more

227
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:55,202
packages and customize your image with
additional content as you desire.

228
00:15:55,202 --> 00:15:59,606
Very similar to what we did when we
use menu-config on buildroot and

229
00:15:59,606 --> 00:16:02,472
change the content of the buildroot image.

230
00:16:02,472 --> 00:16:06,739
This would be where you can customize
what goes into your specific image.

231
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:13,428
So one other thing I wanted to point you
at in case you're having problems getting

232
00:16:13,428 --> 00:16:17,604
started, if you want to just start with
a reference example that's built and

233
00:16:17,604 --> 00:16:20,962
tested and working,
that just includes a really simple app.

234
00:16:20,962 --> 00:16:24,610
In this case, it's just an app
that prints out, hello-world.

235
00:16:24,610 --> 00:16:28,790
The branch and repo here for
yocto-hello-world,

236
00:16:28,790 --> 00:16:34,110
is a reference that you can see
exactly what changes you need to make

237
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:40,956
to the default assignment content in order
to build and test a hello-world example.

238
00:16:40,956 --> 00:16:43,175
See, this might be a helpful reference.

239
00:16:43,175 --> 00:16:45,741
If you're having trouble
getting your build to work,

240
00:16:45,741 --> 00:16:48,359
it might also be one that you
might want to just spin up and

241
00:16:48,359 --> 00:16:51,443
build yourself to prove that your
environment works correctly.

242
00:16:53,327 --> 00:16:56,217
So when we were building with buildroot,

243
00:16:56,217 --> 00:17:00,525
we talked about iterative changes and
the OVERIDE_SRCDIR.

244
00:17:00,525 --> 00:17:05,415
Option for building that lets us
specify where to build from instead

245
00:17:05,415 --> 00:17:08,330
of needing to change a commit harsh, and

246
00:17:08,330 --> 00:17:13,502
referencing an upstream repo every
time we did a change to source code.

247
00:17:13,502 --> 00:17:16,827
There's a similar thing that you
can do with the devtool command.

248
00:17:16,827 --> 00:17:19,518
And the devtool command is
the one I went over earlier.

249
00:17:19,518 --> 00:17:26,788
That is also useful in helping you
set up your initial package recipe.

250
00:17:26,788 --> 00:17:32,544
So using devtool, we can say again,
starting with source pocky/oe-init-build

251
00:17:32,544 --> 00:17:37,256
environment, we can say devtools
modify and then specify a recipe.

252
00:17:37,256 --> 00:17:40,110
And devtool will help us with
modifications to that recipe.

253
00:17:40,110 --> 00:17:43,974
So if I say devtool modify
aesd-assignments, it's going to spit out

254
00:17:43,974 --> 00:17:47,245
a workspace location where
it's copied all of the code.

255
00:17:47,245 --> 00:17:51,054
And you can just do all your editing
from that workspace location and any of

256
00:17:51,054 --> 00:17:55,365
the edits that you make there are going to
automatically get reflected in your build.

257
00:17:55,365 --> 00:18:00,401
So once you make any changes and
have everything working,

258
00:18:00,401 --> 00:18:05,024
you can do the next bitbake,
build or build.sh to use

259
00:18:05,024 --> 00:18:10,500
the source instead of the commit
hash that you've specified.

260
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:14,753
Once you've got everything working,
you can commit and

261
00:18:14,753 --> 00:18:18,384
push your changes from
that working directory,

262
00:18:18,384 --> 00:18:23,446
which again is specified here in
the output of the devtool command.

263
00:18:23,446 --> 00:18:24,746
You'll make all your changes there.

264
00:18:24,746 --> 00:18:28,185
You'll commit and
push from that location and

265
00:18:28,185 --> 00:18:33,917
then go back to your main recipe and
update the hash in your aesd-assignments

266
00:18:33,917 --> 00:18:38,427
.bb file to point to the latest
working and tested changes.

267
00:18:38,427 --> 00:18:43,029
Then, if you do devtool,
reset with your recipe that says ignore

268
00:18:43,029 --> 00:18:48,399
the changes in my workspace and go back
to the content that I have in my layer.

269
00:18:48,399 --> 00:18:53,209
So this will let you switch back from
a mode where you're only building and

270
00:18:53,209 --> 00:18:57,713
testing locally to one that you're
changing in the image content and

271
00:18:57,713 --> 00:19:00,836
what you're sharing on
your assignment repo.

272
00:19:04,553 --> 00:19:09,463
So that's pretty much it for the overview
of the Yocto build sequence for

273
00:19:09,463 --> 00:19:10,680
assignment six.

274
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:15,047
We'll get into some of the other specific
details to assignment six in follow up

275
00:19:15,047 --> 00:19:15,578
videos.

276
00:19:15,578 --> 00:19:19,234
But this should give you an overview and
idea about how you're

277
00:19:19,234 --> 00:19:22,901
going to be able to do your first
Yocto build for assignment six.